  "landfallEstimate": "Movement towards the coast",
  "rawBulletin": "REGIONAL SPECIALISED METEOROLOGICAL CENTRE -TROPICAL CYCLONES,\nNEW DELHI TROPICAL CYCLONE ADVISORY NO 21\nDEMS-RSMC TROPICAL CYCLONES NEW DELHI DATED 19.11.2026\nFROM: RSMC \u2013TROPICAL CYCLONES, NEW DELHI\nTO: STORM WARNING CENTRE, NAYPYITAW (MYANMAR)\nSTORM WARNING CENTRE, BANGKOK (THAILAND)\nSTORM WARNING CENTRE, COLOMBO (SRILANKA)\nSTORM WARNING CENTRE, DHAKA (BANGLADESH)\nSTORM WARNING CENTRE, KARACHI (PAKISTAN)\nMETEOROLOGICAL OFFICE, MALE (MALDIVES)\nOMAN METEOROLOGICAL DEPARTMENT,\nMUSCAT (THROUGH RTH JEDDAH)\nYEMEN METEOROLOGICAL SERVICES,\nREPUBLIC OF YEMEN (THROUGH RTH JEDDAH)\nNATIONAL CENTRE FOR METEOROLOGY, UAE (THROUGH RTH JEDDAH)\nPRESIDENCY OF METEOROLOGY AND ENVIRONMENT,\nSAUDI ARABIA (THROUGH RTH JEDDAH)\nIRAN METEOROLOGICAL ORGANISATION, (THROUGH RTH JEDDAH)\nQATAR METEOROLOGICAL DEPARTMENT (THROUGH RTH JEDDAH)\nTROPICAL CYCLONE ADVISORY NO. 21 FOR THE NORTH INDIAN OCEAN\n(THE BAY OF BENGAL AND THE ARABIAN SEA) VALID FOR THE NEXT 168\nHOURS ISSUED AT 0430 UTC OF 19.11.2026 BASED ON 0000 UTC OF\n19.11.2026.\nSub: Severe Cyclonic Storm \u201cAkvan\u201d [Pronunciation: Akvan] over the westcentral Bay\nof Bengal [Cyclone Warning for North Andhra Pradesh & Yanam and South Odisha\ncoasts]\nThe Severe Cyclonic Storm \u201cAkvan\u201d [Pronunciation: Akvan] over the westcentral Bay of\nBengal moved northwards with the speed of 12 kmph during past 06 hours and lay centered\nat 0000 UTC of today, the 19th November, 2026 over the same region, near Latitude 14.5\u00b0N\nand Longitude 85.3\u00b0E, about 440 km east-southeast of Narsapur (43187, Andhra Pradesh),\n440 km south-southeast of Kalingapatnam (43105, Andhra Pradesh), 580 km east-northeast\nof Chennai (43279, Tamil Nadu), 530 km south of Gopalpur (43049, Odisha) and 590 km\nsouth of Puri (43053, Odisha).\nIt is likely to move north-northwestward and cross North Andhra Pradesh & Yanam and\nSouth Odisha coasts between Narsapur and Puri, close to east of Gopalpur, by 0600 UTC of\n20th November, 2026 with the maximum sustained wind speed of 90-100 kmph gusting to\n110 kmph.\nCloud distribution: (a) Isolated: <25%, Scattered:25-50%, Broken: 51-75%, Solid:>75%, Convection Intensity: (a) Weak:\nCloud Top Temperature(CTT)>-25\u00b0C, (b) Moderate: CTT:-25\u00b0Cto-40\u00b0C, (c) Intense: CTT: -41\u00b0Cto -70\u00b0C and (d) Very\nIntense::Less than -70\u00b0C Probability of cyclogenesis (formation of depression) :NIL:0%, LOW:1-33%, MODERATE:34-\n66% and HIGH:67-100%\nThe forecast location and intensity of the system is given below:\nDate/Time Position Maximum sustained Category of cyclonic\n(UTC) (Lat. 0N/ long. 0E) surface disturbance\nwind speed (Kmph)\n19.11.26/0000 14.5/85.3 90-100 gusting to 110 Severe Cyclonic Storm\n19.11.26/0600 15.5/85.2 90-100 gusting to 110 Severe Cyclonic Storm\n19.11.26/1200 16.5/85.1 90-100 gusting to 110 Severe Cyclonic Storm\n19.11.26/1800 17.5/85.0 90-100 gusting to 110 Severe Cyclonic Storm\n20.11.26/0000 18.5/85.0 90-100 gusting to 110 Severe Cyclonic Storm\n20.11.26/1200 20.5/85.1 50-60 gusting to 70 Deep Depression\n21.11.26/0000 22.5/85.3 20-30 gusting to 40 Well Marked Low\nPressure Area\nAs per INSAT 3DS, vortex (AKVAN) lay over the Westcentral Bay of Bengal and its\nneighbourhood centered near 14.4N/85.7E. Intensity of the system is characterized as\nT3.0. Associated scattered to broken low and medium-level clouds, with embedded intense\nto very intense convection lay over North and Central Bay (minimum cloud-top temperature\n(CTT) ranges from -70\u00b0C to -90\u00b0C) and moderate to intense convection over south Bay,\nOdisha, Gangetic West Bengal, north Costal Andhra Pradesh, Jharkhand, east Bihar,\nSikkim, Northeast state of Bansladesh and Bhutan (minimum cloud-top temperature (CTT)\nranges from -40\u00b0C to -70\u00b0C)\nThe estimated central pressure is 998 hPa and maximum sustained wind speed is 50 kt\ngusting to 60 kt. High to very high Sea condition is prevailing over Bay of Bengal.\nRemarks:\nMadden Julian Oscillation (MJO) is in phase 3 with amplitude more than 1. It is likely to\nmove across phase 4 with a looping back motion towards phase 3 till 21st November.",
